              "length": 428,
              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to check if the maps \\( g \\) and \\( h \\) are equal for all elements in the set \\( S \\) given that \\( g(f(x)) = h(f(x)) \\). If they are not equal, we need to provide a counterexample.\n\nHere's the step-by-step plan:\n1. Read the number of elements \\( N \\) in the set \\( S \\).\n2. Read the elements of the set \\( S \\) and the corresponding values of the map \\( f \\).\n3. Define two maps \\( g \\) and \\( h \\) such that \\( g(f(x)) = h(f(x)) \\) for all \\( x \\in S \\).\n4. Check if \\( g(x) = h(x) \\) for all \\( x \\in S \\). If they are not equal, provide a counterexample.\n\nLet's implement this in Python:\n\n```python\n# Read the number of elements in the set S\nN = int(input())\n\n# Read the elements of the set S\nS = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n# Read the map f\nf_values = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n# Create the maps g and h\n# We can assume g(x) = f(x) and h(x) = f(x) for simplicity\ng = [f_values[i-1] for i in S]\nh = [f_values[i-1] for i in S]\n\n# Check if g(x) = h(x) for all x in S\nall_equal = all(g[i] == h[i] for i in range(N))\n\n# Output the result\nif all_equal:\n    print(\"Yes\")\nelse:\n    print(\"No\")\n    # Provide a counterexample\n    for i in range(N):\n        if g[i] != h[i]:\n            print(S[i], g[i], h[i])\n            break\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, defines the maps \\( g \\) and \\( h \\), checks if they are equal, and provides a counterexample if they are not.",
