              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to check if the maps \\(g\\) and \\(h\\) are the same for all elements in the set \\(S\\) based on the given map \\(f\\). Specifically, we need to verify if for all \\(x \\in S\\), \\(g(f(x)) = h(f(x))\\) implies \\(g(x) = h(x)\\).\n\nHere’s a step-by-step plan to solve the problem:\n\n1. **Read Input**: Read the size of the set \\(N\\) and the elements of the set \\(S\\) and the map \\(f\\).\n2. **Create a Mapping**: Create a dictionary to map each element \\(a_i\\) to its corresponding \\(b_i\\) as per the map \\(f\\).\n3. **Check Injectivity**: For each element \\(x\\) in \\(S\\), check if all elements that map to the same \\(f(x)\\) have the same image under both \\(g\\) and \\(h\\). If any two elements \\(x_1\\) and \\(x_2\\) such that \\(f(x_1) = f(x_2)\\) but \\(g(x_1) \\neq g(x_2)\\) or \\(h(x_1) \\neq h(x_2)\\), then the condition is not satisfied.\n\nLet's write the Python script to implement this logic:\n\n```python\n# Read the size of the set N\nN = int(input())\n\n# Read the elements of the set S and the map f\nelements = list(map(int, input().split()))\nmapping = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n# Create a dictionary to map each element to its image under f\nf_map = {elements[i]: mapping[i] for i in range(N)}\n\n# Create a dictionary to map each image under f to the set of elements that map to it\nf_images = {}\nfor i in range(N):\n    if mapping[i] not in f_images:\n        f_images[mapping[i]] = []\n    f_images[mapping[i]].append(elements[i])\n\n# Check if the condition is satisfied\nis_injective = True\n\n# For each image under f, check if all elements mapping to it have the same image under g and h\nfor image in f_images.values():\n    if len(image) > 1:  # If there are multiple elements mapping to the same image\n        # Assume g and h are different for simplicity, check if they can be different\n        g_values = set()\n        h_values = set()\n        for elem in image:\n            g_values.add(input().strip())  # Read g(elem)\n            h_values.add(input().strip())  # Read h(elem)\n        \n        if len(g_values) > 1 or len(h_values) > 1 or g_values != h_values:\n            is_injective = False\n            break\n\nif is_injective:\n    print(\"Yes\")\nelse:\n    print(\"No\")\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n1. **Reading Input**: We read the size of the set \\(N\\) and the elements and the map \\(f\\).\n2. **Mapping Creation**: We create a dictionary `f_map` to store the mapping \\(f(a_i) = b_i\\).\n3. **Grouping Elements**: We create another dictionary `f_images` to group elements that map to the same image under \\(f\\).\n4. **Checking Injectivity**: We iterate over the images under \\(f\\). For each image, we read the corresponding \\(g(x)\\) and \\(h(x)\\) values and check if they are the same for all elements in the group. If any two elements have different images under \\(g\\) or \\(h\\), we break and print \"No\". If all elements map to the same value under \\(g\\) and \\(h\\), we continue checking the next image.\n5. **Output**: If the condition holds for all images, we print \"Yes\"; otherwise, we print \"No\".\n\nThis script will correctly determine if the given condition is satisfied based on the input.",
