              "length": 786,
              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine if the given maps \\( f \\) and \\( h \\) are consistent with each other under the given conditions. Specifically, for every \\( x \\) in the set \\( S \\), if \\( g(f(x)) = h(f(x)) \\), it must also be true that \\( g(x) = h(x) \\).\n\n### Steps to Solve the Problem:\n\n1. **Input Reading:**\n   - Read the number of elements \\( N \\).\n   - Read the set \\( S \\) as \\( a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_N \\).\n   - Read the map \\( f \\) as \\( b_1, b_2, \\ldots, b_N \\).\n\n2. **Check Consistency:**\n   - For each element \\( x \\) in \\( S \\), check if \\( g(f(x)) = h(f(x)) \\) implies \\( g(x) = h(x) \\).\n   - To do this, we need to consider if there can be a \\( g \\) and \\( h \\) that agree on \\( f(x) \\) but differ on \\( x \\).\n\n3. **Output:**\n   - If such a counterexample exists, print \"No\".\n   - Otherwise, print \"Yes\".\n\n### Implementation:\n\nLet's write the Python script to achieve this:\n\n```python\n# Read the number of elements N\nN = int(input())\n\n# Read the set S and map f\nS = list(map(int, input().split()))\nf = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n# Create a dictionary to store the mapping of each element in S to its corresponding image under f\nf_map = {S[i]: f[i] for i in range(N)}\n\n# Function to check if there is a counterexample\ndef has_counterexample(S, f, f_map):\n    # Iterate through each element x in S\n    for x in S:\n        # Iterate through each element y in S\n        for y in S:\n            # Create two maps g and h that agree on f(x) but differ on x\n            g = {x: x, y: y}\n            h = {x: y, y: x}\n            \n            # Check if g(f(x)) == h(f(x))\n            if f_map[x] == f_map[y]:\n                # Check if g(x) == h(x)\n                if g[f_map[x]] != h[f_map[x]]:\n                    return True\n    return False\n\n# Check for counterexample\nif has_counterexample(S, f, f_map):\n    print(\"No\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Yes\")\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- The script first reads the number of elements \\( N \\) and the sets \\( S \\) and \\( f \\).\n- It then creates a dictionary `f_map` to store the mapping of each element in \\( S \\) to its image under \\( f \\).\n- The function `has_counterexample` checks if there is any pair of maps \\( g \\) and \\( h \\) that agree on \\( f(x) \\) but differ on \\( x \\).\n- If such a pair exists, it prints \"No\". Otherwise, it prints \"Yes\".\n\n### Example Execution:\nFor the given example:\n- \\( N = 5 \\)\n- \\( S = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] \\)\n- \\( f = [3, 4, 2, 5, 1] \\)\n\nThe script will output:\n```\nYes\n```\n\nThis is because for any \\( x \\) in \\( S \\), if \\( g(f(x)) = h(f(x)) \\), it must also be true that \\( g(x) = h(x) \\), ensuring the consistency of the maps.",
