              "text": "Certainly! Below is a Python script that processes the input text, extracts words with 3 to 6 letters, and outputs them separated by a single space.\n\n```python\n# Read input from user\ninput_text = input()\n\n# Define the delimiters\ndelimiters = [' ', ',', '.']\n\n# Initialize an empty list to store the valid words\nvalid_words = []\n\n# Split the input text into tokens based on the delimiters\ntokens = []\ntemp_word = ''\nfor char in input_text:\n    if char in delimiters:\n        if temp_word:\n            tokens.append(temp_word)\n            temp_word = ''\n    else:\n        temp_word += char\nif temp_word:\n    tokens.append(temp_word)\n\n# Filter the tokens to include only words with 3 to 6 letters\nfor token in tokens:\n    if 3 <= len(token) <= 6:\n        valid_words.append(token)\n\n# Join the valid words with a single space and print the result\nprint(' '.join(valid_words))\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n1. **Input Reading:** The script reads a single line of input using `input()`.\n2. **Delimiter Handling:** We define the delimiters as spaces, commas, and periods.\n3. **Tokenization:** The script iterates through each character in the input text. If a delimiter is encountered, it checks if there's a non-empty `temp_word` and appends it to the `tokens` list. It then resets `temp_word`. If a non-delimiter character is encountered, it is added to `temp_word`.\n4. **Filtering:** After tokenizing, the script filters out tokens that have 3 to 6 letters.\n5. **Output:** Finally, it joins the valid words with a single space and prints the result.\n\nThis script will correctly process the input text and output the words with 3 to 6 letters as specified.",
