              "length": 544,
              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the number of times each staff member will step over the cable based on their movement pattern and the position of the cable. We will then sum these values to get the total number of steps over the cable.\n\nHere's the Python script to solve the problem:\n\n```python\n# Read input\nW, H, N = map(int, input().split())\ncable_start = list(map(int, input().split()))\ncable_end = list(map(int, input().split()))\nstaff_positions = []\nstaff_patterns = []\n\n# Read staff positions and patterns\nfor _ in range(N):\n    x, y = map(int, input().split())\n    T, pattern = map(str, input().split())\n    T = int(T)\n    staff_positions.append((x, y))\n    staff_patterns.append((T, pattern))\n\n# Function to calculate the number of times a staff member steps over the cable\ndef calculate_steps(cable_start, cable_end, x, y, T, pattern):\n    # Unpack the pattern\n    pattern = pattern * T\n    steps = 0\n    x_pos, y_pos = x, y\n    for move in pattern:\n        if move == 'U':\n            y_pos -= 1\n        elif move == 'D':\n            y_pos += 1\n        elif move == 'L':\n            x_pos -= 1\n        elif move == 'R':\n            x_pos += 1\n        # Check if the staff is on the cable\n        if (x_pos, y_pos) == (cable_start[0], cable_start[1]):\n            steps += 1\n        elif (x_pos, y_pos) == (cable_end[0], cable_end[1]):\n            steps += 1\n        # Ensure the staff stays within the grid\n        if x_pos < 0:\n            x_pos = 0\n        elif x_pos >= W:\n            x_pos = W - 1\n        if y_pos < 0:\n            y_pos = 0\n        elif y_pos >= H:\n            y_pos = H - 1\n    return steps\n\n# Calculate total steps for all staff\ntotal_steps = 0\nfor x, y in staff_positions:\n    T, pattern = staff_patterns.pop(0)\n    total_steps += calculate_steps(cable_start, cable_end, x, y, T, pattern)\n\n# Output the result\nprint(total_steps)\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, processes each staff member's movement pattern, and calculates the number of times they step over the cable. Finally, it outputs the total number of steps.",
