              "text": "Certainly! Let's break down the problem and then write the Python script to solve it.\n\n### Problem Breakdown\n- You are given a sequence of heights of squares.\n- You start from any square and move to the next square to the right as long as the height of the next square is not greater than the current square.\n- The goal is to find the maximum number of times you can move.\n\n### Approach\n1. Read the number of squares `N` and the heights of the squares.\n2. Iterate through the heights to find the longest sequence where each height is not greater than the previous one.\n3. The length of this sequence minus one will be the maximum number of moves.\n\n### Python Script\n```python\n# Read the number of squares\nN = int(input())\n\n# Read the heights of the squares\nheights = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n# Initialize the maximum number of moves\nmax_moves = 0\n\n# Iterate through the heights to find the longest sequence\nfor i in range(N):\n    moves = 0\n    current_height = heights[i]\n    for j in range(i, N-1):\n        if heights[j+1] <= current_height:\n            moves += 1\n            current_height = heights[j+1]\n        else:\n            break\n    max_moves = max(max_moves, moves)\n\n# Output the result\nprint(max_moves)\n```\n\n### Explanation\n- We read the number of squares `N` and the heights of the squares.\n- We iterate through each square and count the number of moves we can make starting from that square.\n- We keep track of the maximum number of moves.\n- Finally, we output the maximum number of moves.\n\nThis script will correctly handle the given constraints and examples.",
