              "text": "Sure, let's break down the problem and write the Python script step by step.\n\n### Problem Breakdown\n1. **Input**: \n   - First line: Number of squares, \\( N \\).\n   - Second line: Heights of the squares, \\( H_1, H_2, \\ldots, H_N \\).\n\n2. **Objective**: \n   - Start on any square.\n   - Move to the next square if the height of the next square is not greater than the current square.\n   - Find the maximum number of moves you can make.\n\n3. **Constraints**:\n   - \\( 1 \\leq N \\leq 10^5 \\)\n   - \\( 1 \\leq H_i \\leq 10^9 \\)\n\n4. **Output**:\n   - Maximum number of times you can move.\n\n### Steps to Solve\n1. Read the input values.\n2. Iterate through the heights of the squares.\n3. Count the number of moves you can make starting from each square.\n4. Track the maximum number of moves.\n\n### Python Script\n```python\n# Read the number of squares\nN = int(input())\n\n# Read the heights of the squares\nH = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n# Initialize the maximum number of moves\nmax_moves = 0\n\n# Iterate through each square to start from\nfor i in range(N):\n    current_height = H[i]\n    moves = 0\n    \n    # Move to the next square\n    for j in range(i + 1, N):\n        if H[j] <= current_height:\n            moves += 1\n            current_height = H[j]\n        else:\n            break\n    \n    # Update the maximum number of moves\n    max_moves = max(max_moves, moves)\n\n# Output the result\nprint(max_moves)\n```\n\n### Explanation\n1. **Reading Input**: \n   - `N` is the number of squares.\n   - `H` is a list of the heights of the squares.\n\n2. **Iterating and Counting Moves**:\n   - For each starting square, count the number of valid moves.\n   - Use a nested loop to check the next squares and increment the move count until a taller square is found.\n   - Update the maximum number of moves.\n\n3. **Output**: \n   - Print the maximum number of moves.\n\nThis script should correctly solve the problem based on the provided input.",
