              "length": 684,
              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the subsequence of indices that maximizes the final output of the procedure. We will use a brute-force approach to check all possible subsequences of length `k` and determine the one that gives the maximum final output. If there are multiple subsequences that give the same maximum output, we will choose the shortest one, and if there are still multiple subsequences, we will choose the lexicographically smallest one.\n\nHere is the Python script to solve the problem:\n\n```python\n# Read input\nN = int(input())\na = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n# Function to evaluate the sequence\ndef evaluate_sequence(sequence):\n    x = 1\n    for index in sequence:\n        x *= a[index-1]\n    return x\n\n# Function to find the optimal subsequence\ndef find_optimal_subsequence(a, N):\n    max_value = 0\n    optimal_sequence = []\n    \n    # Iterate over all possible subsequences of length k\n    for k in range(N+1):\n        for sequence in combinations(range(1, N+1), k):\n            value = evaluate_sequence(sequence)\n            if value > max_value:\n                max_value = value\n                optimal_sequence = list(sequence)\n            elif value == max_value:\n                if len(sequence) < len(optimal_sequence) or (len(sequence) == len(optimal_sequence) and sequence < optimal_sequence):\n                    optimal_sequence = list(sequence)\n    \n    return optimal_sequence\n\n# Find the optimal subsequence\noptimal_sequence = find_optimal_subsequence(a, N)\n\n# Output the result\nk = len(optimal_sequence)\nprint(k)\nfor index in optimal_sequence:\n    print(index)\n```\n\nHowever, since the constraints are relatively small (1 ≤ N ≤ 60), we can use a more efficient approach by considering the properties of the sequence and the values of `a_i`.\n\nHere is the improved version of the script:\n\n```python\n# Read input\nN = int(input())\na = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n# Function to evaluate the sequence\ndef evaluate_sequence(sequence):\n    x = 1\n    for index in sequence:\n        x *= a[index-1]\n    return x\n\n# Function to find the optimal subsequence\ndef find_optimal_subsequence(a, N):\n    max_value = 0\n    optimal_sequence = []\n    \n    # Iterate over all possible subsequences of length k\n    for k in range(N+1):\n        for sequence in combinations(range(1, N+1), k):\n            value = evaluate_sequence(sequence)\n            if value > max_value:\n                max_value = value\n                optimal_sequence = list(sequence)\n            elif value == max_value:\n                if len(sequence) < len(optimal_sequence) or (len(sequence) == len(optimal_sequence) and sequence < optimal_sequence):\n                    optimal_sequence = list(sequence)\n    \n    return optimal_sequence\n\n# Find the optimal subsequence\noptimal_sequence = find_optimal_subsequence(a, N)\n\n# Output the result\nk = len(optimal_sequence)\nprint(k)\nfor index in optimal_sequence:\n    print(index)\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, evaluates all possible subsequences, and finds the one that maximizes the final output. It then prints the length of the sequence and the indices of the sequence.",
