              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Read the number of formulae \\( N \\) and the coefficients \\( a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_N \\).\n2. Identify the indices of the formulae that will maximize the final output \\( x_{s_k} \\).\n3. Ensure that the sequence of indices is the shortest and lexicographically smallest among all possible sequences that maximize the output.\n\nHere is the Python script to solve the problem:\n\n```python\n# Read the number of formulae\nN = int(input())\n\n# Read the coefficients\ncoefficients = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n# Initialize variables\nmax_value = float('-inf')\nbest_indices = []\n\n# Iterate over all possible subsequences of length k\nfor k in range(1, N + 1):\n    for indices in itertools.combinations(range(N), k):\n        # Initialize x_0\n        x = 1\n        # Evaluate the subsequence\n        for index in indices:\n            x *= coefficients[index]\n        # Check if this subsequence gives a better or equal value\n        if x > max_value:\n            max_value = x\n            best_indices = list(indices)\n        # If the value is the same, check if the sequence is lexicographically smaller\n        elif x == max_value:\n            if len(indices) < len(best_indices) or (len(indices) == len(best_indices) and indices < best_indices):\n                best_indices = list(indices)\n\n# Output the length of the best subsequence\nprint(len(best_indices))\n# Output the indices of the best subsequence\nfor index in best_indices:\n    print(index + 1)  # +1 to convert from 0-based to 1-based index\n```\n\nTo run this script, you need to have the `itertools` module available, which is part of the Python standard library. Here is a complete example with the necessary imports:\n\n```python\nimport itertools\n\n# Read the number of formulae\nN = int(input())\n\n# Read the coefficients\ncoefficients = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n# Initialize variables\nmax_value = float('-inf')\nbest_indices = []\n\n# Iterate over all possible subsequences of length k\nfor k in range(1, N + 1):\n    for indices in itertools.combinations(range(N), k):\n        # Initialize x_0\n        x = 1\n        # Evaluate the subsequence\n        for index in indices:\n            x *= coefficients[index]\n        # Check if this subsequence gives a better or equal value\n        if x > max_value:\n            max_value = x\n            best_indices = list(indices)\n        # If the value is the same, check if the sequence is lexicographically smaller\n        elif x == max_value:\n            if len(indices) < len(best_indices) or (len(indices) == len(best_indices) and indices < best_indices):\n                best_indices = list(indices)\n\n# Output the length of the best subsequence\nprint(len(best_indices))\n# Output the indices of the best subsequence\nfor index in best_indices:\n    print(index + 1)  # +1 to convert from 0-based to 1-based index\n```\n\nThis script will read the input, compute the best subsequence of indices, and output the length of the subsequence followed by the indices.",
