              "contract_reward": 0.1,
              "length": 293,
              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the area of a polygon given its vertices. The vertices are provided in a clockwise order, and the polygon is not necessarily convex. We can use the Shoelace formula (also known as Gauss's area formula) to calculate the area of the polygon.\n\nHere is the Python script to solve the problem:\n\n```python\ndef area_of_polygon(vertices):\n    n = len(vertices)\n    area = 0.0\n    for i in range(n):\n        x1, y1 = vertices[i]\n        x2, y2 = vertices[(i + 1) % n]\n        area += x1 * y2 - y1 * x2\n    return abs(area) / 2.0\n\ndata_sets = []\nn = int(input())\nwhile n != 0:\n    vertices = []\n    for _ in range(n):\n        x, y = map(int, input().split())\n        vertices.append((x, y))\n    data_sets.append(vertices)\n    n = int(input())\n\nsequence_number = 1\nfor vertices in data_sets:\n    area = area_of_polygon(vertices)\n    print(sequence_number, '{:.1f}'.format(area))\n    sequence_number += 1\n```\n\nThis script reads the input data sets, calculates the area of each polygon using the Shoelace formula, and prints the sequence number and the area with one digit to the right of the decimal point.",
