              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to implement a breadth-first search (BFS) algorithm to find the minimum number of moves required to move the king piece to the upper-left corner. Here's a step-by-step plan:\n\n1. Parse the input to get the grid dimensions and the initial placement of pieces.\n2. Identify the positions of the king piece and the open squares.\n3. Use BFS to explore all possible moves and keep track of the minimum number of moves required to reach the goal.\n4. Output the result for each dataset.\n\nLet's implement this in Python:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef parse_input():\n    while True:\n        H, W = map(int, input().split())\n        if H == 0 and W == 0:\n            break\n        grid = [input() for _ in range(H)]\n        return H, W, grid\n\ndef find_pieces(grid):\n    king_pos = None\n    open_squares = []\n    for i in range(len(grid)):\n        for j in range(len(grid[0])):\n            if grid[i][j] == 'o':\n                if king_pos is None:\n                    king_pos = (i, j)\n                else:\n                    return -1  # More than one king piece\n            elif grid[i][j] == '.':\n                open_squares.append((i, j))\n    if len(open_squares) != 2:\n        return -1  # Incorrect number of open squares\n    return king_pos, open_squares\n\ndef bfs(H, W, grid, king_pos, open_squares):\n    directions = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]\n    visited = set()\n    queue = deque([(king_pos, 0)])\n    visited.add(king_pos)\n    \n    while queue:\n        pos, moves = queue.popleft()\n        if pos == (0, 0):\n            return moves\n        for dx, dy in directions:\n            new_pos = (pos[0] + dx, pos[1] + dy)\n            if 0 <= new_pos[0] < H and 0 <= new_pos[1] < W and grid[new_pos[0]][new_pos[1]] != 'X' and new_pos not in visited:\n                if grid[new_pos[0]][new_pos[1]] == 'o':\n                    for open_square in open_squares:\n                        if (new_pos[0] + dx, new_pos[1] + dy) == open_square:\n                            queue.append((new_pos, moves + 1))\n                            visited.add(new_pos)\n                            break\n                elif grid[new_pos[0]][new_pos[1]] == '.':\n                    queue.append((new_pos, moves + 1))\n                    visited.add(new_pos)\n    return -1\n\nwhile True:\n    H, W, grid = parse_input()\n    if H == 0 and W == 0:\n        break\n    king_pos, open_squares = find_pieces(grid)\n    if king_pos is None:\n        print(-1)\n    else:\n        result = bfs(H, W, grid, king_pos, open_squares)\n        print(result)\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, processes each dataset, and outputs the minimum number of moves required to move the king piece to the upper-left corner. If it's not possible, it outputs -1.",
