              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to implement a breadth-first search (BFS) algorithm to find the minimum number of moves required to move the king piece to the upper-left corner of the frame. Here's a step-by-step plan for the Python script:\n\n1. **Read the input**: Read the dimensions of the frame and the initial placement of pieces.\n2. **Represent the state**: Use a tuple or a custom class to represent the state of the puzzle, including the positions of the king and pawn pieces.\n3. **Define the neighbors**: Identify the possible moves for the king and pawn pieces.\n4. **BFS algorithm**: Use BFS to explore all possible states and find the shortest path to the goal state.\n5. **Output the result**: Print the minimum number of moves or -1 if the goal state is unreachable.\n\nHere's the Python script:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef parse_input():\n    while True:\n        H, W = map(int, input().split())\n        if H == 0 and W == 0:\n            break\n        board = [input() for _ in range(H)]\n        yield H, W, board\n\ndef get_state(board):\n    king = []\n    pawn = []\n    for i in range(len(board)):\n        for j in range(len(board[i])):\n            if board[i][j] == 'X':\n                king.append((i, j))\n            elif board[i][j] == 'o':\n                pawn.append((i, j))\n    return king, pawn\n\ndef get_neighbors(king, pawn, board):\n    neighbors = []\n    # Check all possible moves for the king and pawn\n    for (ki, kj) in king:\n        for (pi, pj) in pawn:\n            # Move king\n            if board[ki][kj] == 'X':\n                if board[ki-1][kj] == '.' and board[ki+1][kj] == '.':\n                    neighbors.append(((ki-1, kj), (pi, pj)))\n                if board[ki][kj-1] == '.' and board[ki][kj+1] == '.':\n                    neighbors.append(((ki, kj-1), (pi, pj)))\n                if board[ki-1][kj-1] == '.' and board[ki+1][kj+1] == '.':\n                    neighbors.append(((ki-1, kj-1), (pi, pj)))\n                if board[ki+1][kj-1] == '.' and board[ki-1][kj+1] == '.':\n                    neighbors.append(((ki+1, kj-1), (pi, pj)))\n            # Move pawn\n            if board[pi][pj] == 'o':\n                if board[pi-1][pj] == '.':\n                    neighbors.append((king, ((pi-1, pj), pj)))\n                if board[pi][pj-1] == '.':\n                    neighbors.append((king, ((pi, pj-1), pj)))\n                if board[pi+1][pj] == '.':\n                    neighbors.append((king, ((pi+1, pj), pj)))\n                if board[pi][pj+1] == '.':\n                    neighbors.append((king, ((pi, pj+1), pj)))\n    return neighbors\n\ndef bfs(king, pawn, board):\n    visited = set()\n    queue = deque([(king, pawn, 0)])\n    while queue:\n        king, pawn, moves = queue.popleft()\n        if king == ((0, 0),):\n            return moves\n        for new_king, new_pawn in get_neighbors(king, pawn, board):\n            if (new_king, new_pawn) not in visited:\n                visited.add((new_king, new_pawn))\n                queue.append((new_king, new_pawn, moves + 1))\n    return -1\n\nfor H, W, board in parse_input():\n    king, pawn = get_state(board)\n    result = bfs(king, pawn, board)\n    print(result)\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, processes the board, and uses BFS to find the minimum number of moves to move the king piece to the upper-left corner. If the goal is unreachable, it outputs -1.",
