              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to simulate the movements of the pieces in the puzzle. We will use a breadth-first search (BFS) approach to find the minimum number of moves required to move the king piece to the upper-left corner. Here is the Python script to achieve this:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\n# Function to check if a move is valid\ndef is_valid_move(x, y, board):\n    if x < 0 or x >= len(board) or y < 0 or y >= len(board[0]):\n        return False\n    return board[x][y] == '.' or board[x][y] == 'o'\n\n# Function to get the position of the king piece\ndef get_king_position(board):\n    for i in range(len(board)):\n        for j in range(len(board[0])):\n            if board[i][j] == 'X':\n                return (i, j)\n\n# Function to get the positions of the pawn pieces\ndef get_pawn_positions(board):\n    positions = []\n    for i in range(len(board)):\n        for j in range(len(board[0])):\n            if board[i][j] == 'o':\n                positions.append((i, j))\n    return positions\n\n# Function to perform BFS\ndef bfs(board):\n    king_x, king_y = get_king_position(board)\n    pawn_positions = get_pawn_positions(board)\n    queue = deque([(king_x, king_y, pawn_positions, 0)])\n    visited = set()\n    visited.add((king_x, king_y, frozenset(pawn_positions)))\n    \n    while queue:\n        x, y, pawn_positions, moves = queue.popleft()\n        \n        # Check if the king is at the upper-left corner\n        if x == 0 and y == 0:\n            return moves\n        \n        # Try moving the king piece\n        for dx, dy in [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]:\n            new_x, new_y = x + dx, y + dy\n            if is_valid_move(new_x, new_y, board) and (new_x, new_y, frozenset(pawn_positions)) not in visited:\n                visited.add((new_x, new_y, frozenset(pawn_positions)))\n                queue.append((new_x, new_y, pawn_positions, moves + 1))\n        \n        # Try moving the pawn pieces\n        for i, (px, py) in enumerate(pawn_positions):\n            for dx, dy in [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]:\n                new_px, new_py = px + dx, py + dy\n                if is_valid_move(new_px, new_py, board) and (x, y, frozenset([(new_px, new_py)] + pawn_positions[:i] + pawn_positions[i+1:])) not in visited:\n                    visited.add((x, y, frozenset([(new_px, new_py)] + pawn_positions[:i] + pawn_positions[i+1:])))\n                    queue.append((x, y, [(new_px, new_py)] + pawn_positions[:i] + pawn_positions[i+1:], moves + 1))\n    \n    return -1\n\n# Main function to process the input and output the result\ndef main():\n    while True:\n        H, W = map(int, input().split())\n        if H == 0 and W == 0:\n            break\n        board = [input() for _ in range(H)]\n        result = bfs(board)\n        print(result)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, processes each dataset, and outputs the minimum number of moves required to move the king piece to the upper-left corner. If there is no way to do so, it outputs -1.",
