              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to implement a breadth-first search (BFS) algorithm to find the minimum number of moves required to move the king piece to the upper-left corner of the frame. Here's a step-by-step plan for the Python script:\n\n1. **Read the Input**: Read the height and width of the frame, and the initial placement of pieces.\n2. **Initialize the State**: Represent the initial state of the puzzle.\n3. **Define the Moves**: Define the possible moves for both the king piece and the pawn pieces.\n4. **BFS Algorithm**: Use BFS to explore all possible moves and find the minimum number of moves to reach the goal state.\n5. **Check for Goal State**: Check if the king piece is at the upper-left corner.\n6. **Output the Result**: Print the minimum number of moves or -1 if the goal state is unreachable.\n\nHere's the Python script to solve the problem:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef parse_input():\n    while True:\n        H, W = map(int, input().split())\n        if H == 0 and W == 0:\n            break\n        board = [input() for _ in range(H)]\n        yield H, W, board\n\ndef get_king_position(board):\n    for i in range(len(board)):\n        for j in range(len(board[i])):\n            if board[i][j] == 'X':\n                return (i, j)\n\ndef get_pawn_positions(board):\n    positions = []\n    for i in range(len(board)):\n        for j in range(len(board[i])):\n            if board[i][j] == 'o':\n                positions.append((i, j))\n    return positions\n\ndef is_valid_move(board, new_position):\n    i, j = new_position\n    return 0 <= i < len(board) and 0 <= j < len(board[0]) and board[i][j] != '*'\n\ndef get_valid_moves(board, position):\n    i, j = position\n    moves = []\n    if is_valid_move(board, (i-1, j)) and board[i-1][j] == '.':\n        moves.append((i-1, j))\n    if is_valid_move(board, (i+1, j)) and board[i+1][j] == '.':\n        moves.append((i+1, j))\n    if is_valid_move(board, (i, j-1)) and board[i][j-1] == '.':\n        moves.append((i, j-1))\n    if is_valid_move(board, (i, j+1)) and board[i][j+1] == '.':\n        moves.append((i, j+1))\n    return moves\n\ndef get_king_valid_moves(board, king_position):\n    i, j = king_position\n    moves = []\n    if is_valid_move(board, (i-1, j)) and board[i-1][j] == '.':\n        moves.append((i-1, j))\n    if is_valid_move(board, (i+1, j)) and board[i+1][j] == '.':\n        moves.append((i+1, j))\n    if is_valid_move(board, (i, j-1)) and board[i][j-1] == '.':\n        moves.append((i, j-1))\n    if is_valid_move(board, (i, j+1)) and board[i][j+1] == '.':\n        moves.append((i, j+1))\n    if is_valid_move(board, (i-1, j-1)) and board[i-1][j-1] == '.':\n        moves.append((i-1, j-1))\n    if is_valid_move(board, (i-1, j+1)) and board[i-1][j+1] == '.':\n        moves.append((i-1, j+1))\n    if is_valid_move(board, (i+1, j-1)) and board[i+1][j-1] == '.':\n        moves.append((i+1, j-1))\n    if is_valid_move(board, (i+1, j+1)) and board[i+1][j+1] == '.':\n        moves.append((i+1, j+1))\n    return moves\n\ndef bfs(board, king_position, pawn_positions):\n    queue = deque([(king_position, pawn_positions, 0)])\n    visited = set()\n    while queue:\n        king_position, pawn_positions, moves = queue.popleft()\n        if king_position == (0, 0):\n            return moves\n        if (king_position, tuple(pawn_positions)) in visited:\n            continue\n        visited.add((king_position, tuple(pawn_positions)))\n        for new_king_position in get_king_valid_moves(board, king_position):\n            new_pawn_positions = pawn_positions[:]\n            for pawn_position in pawn_positions:\n                if is_valid_move(board, (pawn_position[0] + new_king_position[0] - king_position[0], pawn_position[1] + new_king_position[1] - king_position[1])):\n                    new_pawn_positions.remove(pawn_position)\n                    new_pawn_positions.append((pawn_position[0] + new_king_position[0] - king_position[0], pawn_position[1] + new_king_position[1] - king_position[1]))\n                    break\n            queue.append((new_king_position, new_pawn_positions, moves + 1))\n    return -1\n\nfor H, W, board in parse_input():\n    king_position = get_king_position(board)\n    pawn_positions = get_pawn_positions(board)\n    result = bfs(board, king_position, pawn_positions)\n    print(result)\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, processes the board, and uses BFS to find the minimum number of moves to move the king piece to the upper-left corner. If it's not possible, it outputs -1.",
