              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to simulate the movement of the king piece and the pawn pieces in the frame. We will use a breadth-first search (BFS) algorithm to find the minimum number of moves required to move the king piece to the upper-left corner. Here's the Python script to achieve this:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef parse_input():\n    while True:\n        H, W = map(int, input().split())\n        if H == 0 and W == 0:\n            break\n        grid = [input() for _ in range(H)]\n        yield H, W, grid\n\ndef find_pieces(grid):\n    king = []\n    pawns = []\n    for i in range(len(grid)):\n        for j in range(len(grid[0])):\n            if grid[i][j] == 'o':\n                king.append((i, j))\n            elif grid[i][j] == 'X':\n                pawns.append((i, j))\n    return king, pawns\n\ndef get_neighbors(i, j, H, W):\n    neighbors = []\n    for di, dj in [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]:\n        ni, nj = i + di, j + dj\n        if 0 <= ni < H and 0 <= nj < W and grid[ni][nj] == '.':\n            neighbors.append((ni, nj))\n    return neighbors\n\ndef bfs(H, W, grid, king, pawns):\n    queue = deque([(king, pawns, 0)])\n    visited = set([king + tuple(sorted(pawns))])\n    \n    while queue:\n        current_king, current_pawns, moves = queue.popleft()\n        \n        if current_king[0] == 0 and current_king[1] == 0:\n            return moves\n        \n        for i, j in current_king:\n            for ni, nj in get_neighbors(i, j, H, W):\n                new_king = [(ni, nj)] + [(x, y) for x, y in current_king if (x, y) != (i, j)]\n                new_pawns = current_pawns\n                if (ni, nj) in current_pawns:\n                    new_pawns.remove((ni, nj))\n                if tuple(sorted(new_king + new_pawns)) not in visited:\n                    visited.add(tuple(sorted(new_king + new_pawns)))\n                    queue.append((new_king, new_pawns, moves + 1))\n        \n        for i, j in current_pawns:\n            for ni, nj in get_neighbors(i, j, H, W):\n                new_pawns = current_pawns[:]\n                new_pawns.remove((i, j))\n                new_pawns.append((ni, nj))\n                if tuple(sorted(new_king + new_pawns)) not in visited:\n                    visited.add(tuple(sorted(new_king + new_pawns)))\n                    queue.append((current_king, new_pawns, moves + 1))\n    \n    return -1\n\nfor H, W, grid in parse_input():\n    king, pawns = find_pieces(grid)\n    result = bfs(H, W, grid, king, pawns)\n    print(result)\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, processes each dataset, and uses a breadth-first search to find the minimum number of moves to move the king piece to the upper-left corner. If it's not possible, it outputs -1.",
