              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to implement a pathfinding algorithm that can navigate through the grid, considering the movement rules of the king and pawn pieces. We will use a breadth-first search (BFS) algorithm to find the shortest path from the initial position of the king piece to the upper-left corner of the frame.\n\nHere is the Python script to solve the problem:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef is_valid_move(x, y, H, W, grid):\n    return 0 <= x < H and 0 <= y < W and grid[x][y] != 'X' and grid[x][y] != 'X*'\n\ndef get_neighbors(x, y, H, W, grid):\n    neighbors = []\n    # Check all possible moves for the king piece\n    for dx, dy in [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]:\n        nx, ny = x + dx, y + dy\n        if is_valid_move(nx, ny, H, W, grid):\n            if grid[nx][ny] == '*' or grid[nx][ny] == 'o':\n                neighbors.append((nx, ny, 1))  # 1 is the cost for moving the king piece\n            else:\n                neighbors.append((nx, ny, 0))  # 0 is the cost for moving a pawn piece\n\n    # Check possible moves for the king piece\n    if is_valid_move(x, y - 1, H, W, grid) and is_valid_move(x, y + 1, H, W, grid):\n        for dx, dy in [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]:\n            nx, ny = x + dx, y + dy\n            if is_valid_move(nx, ny, H, W, grid):\n                if grid[nx][ny] == '*' or grid[nx][ny] == 'o':\n                    neighbors.append((nx, ny, 1))\n                else:\n                    neighbors.append((nx, ny, 0))\n    return neighbors\n\ndef bfs(H, W, grid):\n    start = None\n    end = None\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == 'X':\n                start = (i, j, 0)  # (x, y, cost)\n            elif grid[i][j] == '.':\n                end = (i, j, 0)  # (x, y, cost)\n    if start is None or end is None:\n        return -1\n    \n    queue = deque([start])\n    visited = set([start])\n    while queue:\n        x, y, cost = queue.popleft()\n        if (x, y) == end:\n            return cost\n        \n        for nx, ny, move_cost in get_neighbors(x, y, H, W, grid):\n            if (nx, ny) not in visited:\n                visited.add((nx, ny))\n                queue.append((nx, ny, cost + move_cost))\n    \n    return -1\n\nwhile True:\n    H, W = map(int, input().split())\n    if H == 0 and W == 0:\n        break\n    grid = []\n    for _ in range(H):\n        grid.append(input().strip())\n    print(bfs(H, W, grid))\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, constructs the grid, and uses BFS to find the shortest path from the king piece's starting position to the upper-left corner. It handles the movement rules for both the king and pawn pieces and outputs the minimum number of moves required or -1 if it's not possible.",
