              "contract_reward": 0.1,
              "length": 347,
              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to perform the frequency operation repeatedly until the sequence no longer changes. We will use a loop to perform the frequency operation and keep track of the number of operations. Once the sequence stabilizes, we will output the sequence and the number of operations.\n\nHere is the Python script to solve the problem:\n\n```python\ndef frequency_operation(sequence):\n    frequency_dict = {}\n    for number in sequence:\n        if number in frequency_dict:\n            frequency_dict[number] += 1\n        else:\n            frequency_dict[number] = 1\n    \n    result = [frequency_dict[number] for number in sequence]\n    return result\n\ndef find_fixed_point(n, sequence):\n    operations = 0\n    while True:\n        new_sequence = frequency_operation(sequence)\n        if new_sequence == sequence:\n            break\n        sequence = new_sequence\n        operations += 1\n    \n    return operations, new_sequence\n\n# Read input\nwhile True:\n    n = int(input())\n    if n == 0:\n        break\n    sequence = list(map(int, input().split()))\n    \n    operations, fixed_point = find_fixed_point(n, sequence)\n    print(operations)\n    print(' '.join(map(str, fixed_point)))\n```\n\nThis script works as follows:\n1. Define a function `frequency_operation` that takes a sequence and returns the frequency sequence.\n2. Define a function `find_fixed_point` that repeatedly applies the frequency operation until the sequence stabilizes, and returns the number of operations and the fixed point sequence.\n3. Read the input, process each dataset, and output the results.\n\nThis script will correctly process the input and output the fixed point sequence and the number of operations for each dataset.",
