              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the shortest path that visits each point exactly once and then compute the XOR of the permutation of points that gives this shortest path. Here's a Python script that reads the input, processes it, and outputs the result as described:\n\n```python\nimport itertools\n\ndef calculate_distance(point1, point2):\n    return min(abs(point1[0] - point2[0]), abs(point1[1] - point2[1]))\n\ndef find_shortest_path(points):\n    n = len(points)\n    min_distance = float('inf')\n    best_permutation = []\n\n    # Generate all permutations of points\n    for permutation in itertools.permutations(range(n)):\n        total_distance = 0\n        for i in range(n - 1):\n            total_distance += calculate_distance(points[permutation[i]], points[permutation[i + 1]])\n        total_distance += calculate_distance(points[permutation[-1]], points[permutation[0]])  # Return to start\n        if total_distance < min_distance:\n            min_distance = total_distance\n            best_permutation = permutation\n\n    # If there are multiple permutations with the same distance, choose the lexicographically smallest one\n    if len(best_permutation) > 1:\n        for permutation in itertools.permutations(range(n)):\n            if calculate_distance(points[permutation[0]], points[permutation[1]]) == calculate_distance(points[best_permutation[0]], points[best_permutation[1]]) and permutation < best_permutation:\n                best_permutation = permutation\n\n    # Compute the XOR of the best permutation\n    xor_result = 0\n    for point_index in best_permutation:\n        xor_result ^= point_index + 1  # +1 to convert 0-based index to 1-based index as per the problem statement\n\n    return xor_result\n\n# Read input\nT = int(input())\nresults = []\n\nfor _ in range(T):\n    N = int(input())\n    points = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        px, py = map(int, input().split())\n        points.append((px, py))\n    \n    result = find_shortest_path(points)\n    results.append(result)\n\n# Output results\nfor result in results:\n    print(result)\n```\n\nThis script does the following:\n1. Reads the number of test cases and processes each test case.\n2. For each test case, it reads the number of points and their coordinates.\n3. It generates all permutations of the points and calculates the total distance for each permutation.\n4. It selects the permutation with the minimum distance and, in case of a tie, the lexicographically smallest one.\n5. It computes the XOR of the indices of the points in the best permutation.\n6. Finally, it prints the result for each test case.",
