              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to simulate the movement of the ninja on the polygon and count the minimum number of commands required to reach the goal point. Here's a step-by-step plan for the solution:\n\n1. **Read Input**: Read the number of vertices, start and goal coordinates, and the polygon vertices.\n2. **Initialize State**: Start from the initial position and direction (initially facing the direction to the goal).\n3. **Simulate Movement**: Use a while loop to simulate the movement, updating the position and direction based on the rules provided.\n4. **Count Commands**: Count the number of commands required to reach the goal.\n5. **Repeat for Each Dataset**: Repeat the process for each dataset until the input is zero.\n\nHere's the Python script to implement this:\n\n```python\ndef is_collision(x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3, x4, y4):\n    # Check if line segments (x1, y1) -> (x2, y2) and (x3, y3) -> (x4, y4) intersect\n    def ccw(A, B, C):\n        return (C[1] - A[1]) * (B[0] - A[0]) > (B[1] - A[1]) * (C[0] - A[0])\n    \n    return ccw((x1, y1), (x2, y2), (x3, y4)) != ccw((x3, y3), (x4, y4), (x1, y1)) and \\\n           ccw((x1, y1), (x2, y2), (x3, y3)) != ccw((x3, y3), (x4, y4), (x1, y1))\n\ndef find_min_commands(N, sx, sy, gx, gy, vertices):\n    x, y = sx, sy\n    dir_x, dir_y = 1, 0  # Start facing right\n\n    command_count = 0\n\n    while (x, y) != (gx, gy):\n        next_x, next_y = x + dir_x, y + dir_y\n        if next_x < -10000 or next_x > 10000 or next_y < -10000 or next_y > 10000:\n            # Out of bounds, change direction\n            dir_x, dir_y = 0, 1\n            command_count += 1\n            continue\n\n        collision = False\n        for i in range(N):\n            x1, y1 = vertices[i]\n            x2, y2 = vertices[(i + 1) % N]\n            if is_collision(x, y, next_x, next_y, x1, y1, x2, y2):\n                collision = True\n                break\n\n        if collision:\n            # Check corner behavior\n            for i in range(N):\n                x1, y1 = vertices[i]\n                x2, y2 = vertices[(i + 1) % N]\n                if (x, y) in [(x1, y1), (x2, y2)] and (next_x, next_y) in [(x1, y1), (x2, y2)]:\n                    dir_x, dir_y = 0, 1\n                    command_count += 1\n                    break\n            continue\n\n        x, y = next_x, next_y\n        command_count += 1\n\n    return command_count\n\nwhile True:\n    N = int(input())\n    if N == 0:\n        break\n    sx, sy, gx, gy = map(int, input().split())\n    vertices = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        x, y = map(int, input().split())\n        vertices.append((x, y))\n    print(find_min_commands(N, sx, sy, gx, gy, vertices))\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, processes each dataset, and prints the minimum number of commands required to reach the goal point.",
