              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to simulate the movement of the ninja on the polygon and determine the minimum number of command inputs required to reach the goal point from the start point. Here's a step-by-step plan to achieve this:\n\n1. **Read the Input**: Parse the input to get the number of vertices, the start and goal points, and the coordinates of the vertices.\n2. **Initialize the Ninja's Position**: Start at the given start point.\n3. **Simulate Movement**: Use a while loop to simulate the movement of the ninja. The ninja can move in eight directions: up, down, left, right, and diagonally 45°.\n4. **Check for Movement Conditions**: For each movement, check if the ninja can move along the wall or if it hits a corner. Adjust the movement direction accordingly.\n5. **Count Command Inputs**: Increment the command input count each time a direction is changed.\n6. **Stop Condition**: The loop should stop when the ninja reaches the goal point.\n7. **Output the Result**: Print the minimum number of command inputs required.\n\nHere's the Python script to implement this:\n\n```python\ndef find_min_commands(N, sx, sy, gx, gy, polygon):\n    # Initialize the ninja's position\n    x, y = sx, sy\n    commands = 0\n    \n    # Function to move in a given direction\n    def move(dx, dy):\n        nonlocal x, y, commands\n        while (x + dx, y + dy) in polygon and (x + dx, y + dy) != (gx, gy):\n            x += dx\n            y += dy\n            commands += 1\n        return (x, y)\n    \n    # Function to move diagonally\n    def move_diagonal(dx, dy):\n        nonlocal x, y, commands\n        while (x + dx, y + dy) in polygon and (x + dx, y + dy) != (gx, gy):\n            x += dx\n            y += dy\n            commands += 1\n        return (x, y)\n    \n    # Simulate movement\n    while (x, y) != (gx, gy):\n        # Check if moving in a direction would hit a wall or corner\n        if (x, y + 1) in polygon and (x, y - 1) in polygon and (x + 1, y) in polygon and (x - 1, y) in polygon:\n            # Move in the direction of the goal point\n            if gx > x and gy > y:\n                x, y = move_diagonal(1, 1)\n            elif gx > x and gy < y:\n                x, y = move_diagonal(1, -1)\n            elif gx < x and gy > y:\n                x, y = move_diagonal(-1, 1)\n            elif gx < x and gy < y:\n                x, y = move_diagonal(-1, -1)\n            elif gx == x and gy > y:\n                x, y = move(0, 1)\n            elif gx == x and gy < y:\n                x, y = move(0, -1)\n            elif gx > x and gy == y:\n                x, y = move(1, 0)\n            elif gx < x and gy == y:\n                x, y = move(-1, 0)\n        else:\n            # Move in the direction of the goal point\n            if gx > x and gy > y:\n                x, y = move_diagonal(1, 1)\n            elif gx > x and gy < y:\n                x, y = move_diagonal(1, -1)\n            elif gx < x and gy > y:\n                x, y = move_diagonal(-1, 1)\n            elif gx < x and gy < y:\n                x, y = move_diagonal(-1, -1)\n            elif gx == x and gy > y:\n                x, y = move(0, 1)\n            elif gx == x and gy < y:\n                x, y = move(0, -1)\n            elif gx > x and gy == y:\n                x, y = move(1, 0)\n            elif gx < x and gy == y:\n                x, y = move(-1, 0)\n    \n    return commands\n\nwhile True:\n    N = int(input())\n    if N == 0:\n        break\n    sx, sy, gx, gy = map(int, input().split())\n    polygon = set()\n    for _ in range(N):\n        x, y = map(int, input().split())\n        polygon.add((x, y))\n    print(find_min_commands(N, sx, sy, gx, gy, polygon))\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, processes each dataset, and outputs the minimum number of command inputs required to reach the goal point from the start point.",
