              "length": 675,
              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the time intervals for each watch and then find the shortest interval that all watches can be in. Here's a step-by-step approach:\n\n1. **Read the Input**: We need to read the number of watches and their positions.\n2. **Calculate Possible Times**: For each watch, calculate all possible times by rotating the hands and permuting them.\n3. **Find Overlapping Intervals**: Determine the time intervals that all watches can be in and find the shortest one.\n4. **Output the Result**: Print the shortest interval.\n\nLet's write the Python script to achieve this:\n\n```python\ndef calculate_possible_times(s, t, u):\n    # Calculate the positions of the hands\n    positions = [(s, t, u), (t, u, s), (u, s, t)]\n    times = []\n    for pos in positions:\n        # Convert positions to time\n        hour = pos[0] // 20\n        minute = pos[1] // 6\n        second = pos[2]\n        times.append((hour, minute, second))\n    return times\n\ndef find_shortest_interval(watches):\n    # Calculate all possible times for each watch\n    all_times = []\n    for watch in watches:\n        all_times.extend(calculate_possible_times(*watch))\n    \n    # Sort all times\n    all_times.sort()\n    \n    # Find the shortest interval\n    min_interval = (0, 0, 0)\n    for i in range(len(all_times) - 1):\n        start_time = all_times[i]\n        end_time = all_times[i + 1]\n        if end_time[0] == 0 and end_time[1] == 0 and end_time[2] == 0:\n            continue\n        interval = (start_time, end_time)\n        if interval[0] < interval[1] and (interval[1][0] - interval[0][0] < 12) and (interval[1][1] - interval[0][1] < 60) and (interval[1][2] - interval[0][2] < 60):\n            min_interval = min(min_interval, interval, key=lambda x: (x[1][0], x[1][1], x[1][2]))\n    \n    return min_interval\n\nwhile True:\n    n = int(input())\n    if n == 0:\n        break\n    watches = []\n    for _ in range(n):\n        s, t, u = map(int, input().split())\n        watches.append((s, t, u))\n    \n    min_interval = find_shortest_interval(watches)\n    start_time = f\"{min_interval[0][0]:02}:{min_interval[0][1]:02}:{min_interval[0][2]:02}\"\n    end_time = f\"{min_interval[1][0]:02}:{min_interval[1][1]:02}:{min_interval[1][2]:02}\"\n    print(start_time, end_time)\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, calculates all possible times for each watch, and then finds the shortest interval that all watches can be in. It outputs the result in the required format.",
