              "length": 642,
              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to consider all possible permutations of the hands and rotations of the watch to find the shortest time interval where all watches can be interpreted as having at least one candidate time. Here's a step-by-step approach to achieve this:\n\n1. **Read the input**: The input consists of multiple datasets, each with a number of watches and their positions.\n2. **Process each dataset**: For each dataset, generate all possible permutations of the hands and all possible rotations of the watch.\n3. **Calculate candidate times**: For each permutation and rotation, calculate the time the watch would show.\n4. **Find the shortest interval**: Determine the shortest time interval where all watches have at least one candidate time.\n\nLet's write the Python script to achieve this:\n\n```python\ndef rotate_time(hh, mm, ss, rotation):\n    # Rotate the time by the given rotation\n    new_hh = (hh + rotation) % 12\n    new_mm = (mm + rotation // 2) % 60\n    new_ss = (ss + rotation // 12) % 60\n    return new_hh, new_mm, new_ss\n\ndef generate_times(s, t, u):\n    times = []\n    for hh in range(12):\n        for mm in range(60):\n            for ss in range(60):\n                if (hh * 60 * 60 + mm * 60 + ss) % (12 * 60 * 60) == (s * 6 + t * 6 + u * 6) % (12 * 60 * 60):\n                    times.append((hh, mm, ss))\n    return times\n\ndef find_shortest_interval(times):\n    min_start = None\n    min_end = None\n    for start in times:\n        for end in times:\n            if start <= end:\n                if min_start is None or end - start < min_end - min_start:\n                    min_start = start\n                    min_end = end\n    return min_start, min_end\n\nwhile True:\n    n = int(input())\n    if n == 0:\n        break\n    \n    watches = []\n    for _ in range(n):\n        s, t, u = map(int, input().split())\n        watches.append((s, t, u))\n    \n    all_times = set()\n    for watch in watches:\n        for rotation in range(60):\n            times = generate_times(watch[0], watch[1], watch[2])\n            for time in times:\n                all_times.add(time)\n    \n    min_start, min_end = find_shortest_interval(all_times)\n    print(f\"{min_start[0]:02d}:{min_start[1]:02d}:{min_start[2]:02d} {min_end[0]:02d}:{min_end[1]:02d}:{min_end[2]:02d}\")\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, processes each dataset, and outputs the shortest time interval where all watches have at least one candidate time.",
