              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the time interval during which all the watches can show at least one candidate time. We will follow these steps:\n\n1. Parse the input to extract the positions of the three hands for each watch.\n2. Generate all possible times for each watch by considering all rotations and permutations of the hands.\n3. Determine the shortest time interval that includes all the candidate times of the watches.\n4. Output the interval in the required format.\n\nHere is the Python script to achieve this:\n\n```python\ndef generate_times(positions):\n    times = []\n    for i in range(3):\n        for j in range(3):\n            for k in range(3):\n                if i != j and j != k and i != k:\n                    # Rotate hands to find all possible times\n                    time = [positions[i], positions[j], positions[k]]\n                    times.append(time)\n    return times\n\ndef calculate_time(positions):\n    # Calculate the time based on the positions of the hands\n    # Assuming the positions are in ticks\n    second_hand = positions[0] % 60\n    minute_hand = (positions[1] % 60) // 10 + (positions[1] % 10) / 6\n    hour_hand = (positions[2] % 60) // 10 + (positions[2] % 10) / 6 / 5\n    return (int(hour_hand) % 12, int(minute_hand), int(second_hand))\n\ndef find_shortest_interval(watches):\n    min_start = None\n    max_end = None\n    for watch in watches:\n        times = generate_times(watch)\n        for time in times:\n            hour, minute, second = calculate_time(time)\n            if min_start is None or hour < min_start[0] or (hour == min_start[0] and minute < min_start[1]) or (hour == min_start[0] and minute == min_start[1] and second < min_start[2]):\n                min_start = (hour, minute, second)\n            if max_end is None or hour > max_end[0] or (hour == max_end[0] and minute > max_end[1]) or (hour == max_end[0] and minute == max_end[1] and second > max_end[2]):\n                max_end = (hour, minute, second)\n    return min_start, max_end\n\ndef format_time(hour, minute, second):\n    return f\"{hour:02}:{minute:02}:{second:02}\"\n\nwhile True:\n    n = int(input())\n    if n == 0:\n        break\n    watches = []\n    for _ in range(n):\n        s, t, u = map(int, input().split())\n        watches.append([s, t, u])\n    min_start, max_end = find_shortest_interval(watches)\n    print(f\"{format_time(min_start[0], min_start[1], min_start[2])} {format_time(max_end[0], max_end[1], max_end[2])}\")\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, processes each watch to find all possible times, and then determines the shortest time interval that includes all the candidate times. The results are printed in the required format.",
