              "length": 718,
              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the time interval during which all the given watches could have been stopped. We will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Parse the input**: Read the number of watches and their positions.\n2. **Generate all possible times**: For each watch, generate all possible times by rotating the watch and permuting the hands.\n3. **Find the common time interval**: Determine the shortest time interval that includes all the possible times of all the watches.\n4. **Output the result**: Print the shortest time interval.\n\nHere is the Python script to achieve this:\n\n```python\ndef generate_times(positions):\n    # Generate all possible times from the positions\n    times = set()\n    for i in range(60):\n        for j in range(60):\n            for k in range(60):\n                times.add((positions[0] + i) % 60, (positions[1] + j) % 60, (positions[2] + k) % 60)\n    return times\n\ndef find_shortest_interval(watches):\n    all_times = set()\n    for watch in watches:\n        all_times.update(generate_times(watch))\n    \n    all_times = list(all_times)\n    all_times.sort()\n    \n    min_interval = (12 * 60 * 60, 0, 0)  # 12 hours in seconds\n    start_time = None\n    end_time = None\n    \n    for i in range(len(all_times) - 1):\n        current_time = all_times[i]\n        next_time = all_times[i + 1]\n        \n        time_diff = (next_time[0] * 3600 + next_time[1] * 60 + next_time[2]) - (current_time[0] * 3600 + current_time[1] * 60 + current_time[2])\n        \n        if time_diff < min_interval[0]:\n            min_interval = (time_diff, current_time, next_time)\n            start_time = current_time\n            end_time = next_time\n    \n    return start_time, end_time\n\ndef main():\n    while True:\n        n = int(input())\n        if n == 0:\n            break\n        \n        watches = []\n        for _ in range(n):\n            s, t, u = map(int, input().split())\n            watches.append((s, t, u))\n        \n        start_time, end_time = find_shortest_interval(watches)\n        \n        start_hh = start_time[0] // 3600\n        start_mm = (start_time[0] % 3600) // 60\n        start_ss = start_time[0] % 60\n        \n        end_hh = end_time[0] // 3600\n        end_mm = (end_time[0] % 3600) // 60\n        end_ss = end_time[0] % 60\n        \n        print(f\"{start_hh:02}:{start_mm:02}:{start_ss:02} {end_hh:02}:{end_mm:02}:{end_ss:02}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, processes the watches to generate all possible times, and then finds the shortest time interval that includes all these times. It outputs the result in the required format.",
