              "length": 541,
              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Read the input and parse the glyphs and strings.\n2. Create a dictionary to map glyphs to their corresponding letters.\n3. Process each string and determine if it should be read from left to right or right to left.\n4. Transliterate the string based on the determined direction.\n5. Output the result and a `#` after each dataset.\n\nHere is the Python script to achieve this:\n\n```python\ndef parse_glyph(glyph):\n    c, h, w = map(int, glyph.split())\n    bitmap = []\n    for _ in range(h):\n        row = input()\n        bitmap.append(row)\n    return c, bitmap\n\ndef parse_string(string):\n    h, w = map(int, string.split())\n    bitmap = []\n    for _ in range(h):\n        row = input()\n        bitmap.append(row)\n    return bitmap\n\ndef is_flip(glyph):\n    # Check if the glyph is flipped horizontally\n    flipped = False\n    for i in range(len(glyph[1])):\n        if glyph[1][i] == glyph[1][0] and i != 0:\n            flipped = True\n            break\n    return flipped\n\ndef find_glyph(bitmap, glyphs):\n    for glyph in glyphs:\n        if len(glyph[1]) != len(bitmap):\n            continue\n        if all(glyph[1][i] == bitmap[i] for i in range(len(bitmap))):\n            return glyph\n        if is_flip(glyph) and all(glyph[1][i] == bitmap[i] for i in range(len(bitmap))):\n            return glyph\n    return None\n\ndef transliterate_string(bitmap, glyphs):\n    result = []\n    for row in bitmap:\n        for cell in row:\n            if cell == '*':\n                glyph = find_glyph(row, glyphs)\n                if glyph:\n                    result.append(glyph[0])\n                else:\n                    result.append('[')\n                    result.extend(transliterate_string(row, glyphs))\n                    result.append(']')\n    return ''.join(result)\n\nwhile True:\n    n, m = map(int, input().split())\n    if n == 0 and m == 0:\n        break\n    \n    glyphs = [parse_glyph(input()) for _ in range(n)]\n    strings = [parse_string(input()) for _ in range(m)]\n    \n    for string in strings:\n        result = transliterate_string(string, glyphs)\n        print(result)\n    \n    print('#')\n```\n\nThis script reads the input, processes each string, and transliterates it based on the provided glyphs. It handles the flipping of glyphs and the structure of the sentences as described.",
