              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to process each monolith's sequence based on the given rules. Here's a step-by-step plan for the Python script:\n\n1. **Read Input**: We read the number of glyphs and the number of monoliths.\n2. **Process Glyphs**: For each glyph, read its description and store it.\n3. **Process Monoliths**: For each monolith, read its sequence and determine the transliteration rules.\n4. **Transliteration Rules**:\n   - Identify the direction of the sequence (left-to-right or right-to-left).\n   - Use the stored glyphs to determine the transliteration.\n5. **Output**: Print the transliterated sequence and a `#` to separate different datasets.\n\nHere is the Python script to accomplish the above tasks:\n\n```python\ndef read_glyph():\n    n, m = map(int, input().split())\n    if n == 0 and m == 0:\n        return None, None\n    glyphs = {}\n    for _ in range(n):\n        c, h, w = input().split()\n        h, w = int(h), int(w)\n        bitmap = []\n        for _ in range(h):\n            row = input()\n            bitmap.append(row)\n        glyphs[c] = (h, w, bitmap)\n    monoliths = []\n    for _ in range(m):\n        h, w = map(int, input().split())\n        bitmap = []\n        for _ in range(h):\n            row = input()\n            bitmap.append(row)\n        monoliths.append(bitmap)\n    return glyphs, monoliths\n\ndef find_glyph(bitmap, glyphs):\n    for c, (h, w, bitmap_glyph) in glyphs.items():\n        if (h, w) == (len(bitmap), len(bitmap[0])) and all(bitmap[i][j] == '.*'[bitmap[i][j] == '*'] for i in range(h) for j in range(w)):\n            return c\n    # Check flipped version\n    for c, (h, w, bitmap_glyph) in glyphs.items():\n        if (h, w) == (len(bitmap), len(bitmap[0])) and all(bitmap[i][j] == '.*'[bitmap[i][j] == '*'] for i in range(h) for j in range(w)):\n            flipped_bitmap = [row[::-1] for row in bitmap]\n            if all(flipped_bitmap[i][j] == '.*'[flipped_bitmap[i][j] == '*'] for i in range(h) for j in range(w)):\n                return c[::-1]\n    return None\n\ndef find_sequence(monolith, glyphs):\n    height, width = len(monolith), len(monolith[0])\n    results = []\n    i, j = height - 1, 0\n    while i >= 0 and j < width:\n        glyph = find_glyph(monolith[i][j:], glyphs)\n        if glyph:\n            results.append(glyph)\n            j += len(monolith[i][j:]) - 1\n        elif monolith[i][j] == '*':\n            results.append('[')\n            if j + 1 < width and monolith[i][j+1] == '*':\n                j += 2\n                continue\n            else:\n                results.append(']')\n            j += 1\n        else:\n            j += 1\n        i -= 1\n    if any(c in results for c in glyphs.keys()):\n        # Determine direction based on unreadable glyphs\n        if any(c[::-1] in glyphs for c in results):\n            return ''.join(results[::-1])\n        else:\n            return ''.join(results)\n    else:\n        return ''.join(results[::-1])\n\nwhile True:\n    glyphs, monoliths = read_glyph()\n    if glyphs is None:\n        break\n    for monolith in monoliths:\n        result = find_sequence(monolith, glyphs)\n        print(result)\n    print('#')\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n1. **Reading Input**: The script reads the number of glyphs and monoliths, then reads the glyphs and monoliths bitmaps.\n2. **Finding Glyphs**: The `find_glyph` function checks if a given bitmap matches any stored glyph.\n3. **Finding Sequence**: The `find_sequence` function processes the monolith sequence by finding matching glyphs and determining the direction of the sequence.\n4. **Output**: The script prints the transliterated sequence for each monolith and a `#` to separate different datasets.\n\nThis script should correctly handle the given problem constraints and examples.",
