              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to count the number of ways to separate the string \\( t \\) such that each part is included in the set \\( S \\). We will use dynamic programming to keep track of the number of ways to separate the string up to each position.\n\nHere is the step-by-step plan:\n\n1. **Read the input**: Read the number of strings \\( N \\), the set \\( S \\), and the string \\( t \\).\n2. **Initialize DP array**: Create a DP array where `dp[i]` represents the number of ways to separate the string up to the \\( i \\)-th character of \\( t \\).\n3. **Fill the DP array**: Iterate through each character of \\( t \\) and check if the substring from the start to the current position matches any string in \\( S \\). If it does, update the DP array.\n4. **Output the result**: Print the value of `dp[len(t)]` modulo \\( 1,000,000,007 \\).\n\nLet's implement this in Python:\n\n```python\ndef count_separations():\n    # Read input\n    N = int(input())\n    S = set()\n    for _ in range(N):\n        S.add(input().strip())\n    t = input().strip()\n    \n    # Initialize DP array\n    dp = [0] * (len(t) + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1  # Base case: one way to separate an empty string\n    \n    # Fill the DP array\n    for i in range(1, len(t) + 1):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if t[j:i] in S:\n                dp[i] += dp[j]\n                dp[i] %= 1000000007\n    \n    # Output the result\n    print(dp[len(t)])\n\n# Call the function\ncount_separations()\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n1. **Reading Input**: We read the number of strings \\( N \\) and the set \\( S \\) of strings. We then read the string \\( t \\).\n2. **DP Initialization**: We initialize a DP array `dp` where `dp[i]` will store the number of ways to separate the string up to the \\( i \\)-th character of \\( t \\). We set `dp[0]` to 1 because there is one way to separate an empty string.\n3. **Filling DP Array**: We iterate through each character of \\( t \\) and check all possible substrings ending at the current position. If a substring matches a string in \\( S \\), we update the DP array.\n4. **Output**: We print the value of `dp[len(t)]` modulo \\( 1,000,000,007 \\).\n\nThis approach ensures that we count all valid separations of the string \\( t \\) using the strings in \\( S \\).",
