              "length": 332,
              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to understand the concept of Hamming Distance. The Hamming Distance between two strings of equal length is the number of positions at which the corresponding characters are different. Given a string of length \\( L \\) with all distinct characters, we need to find the number of permutations of the string such that the Hamming Distance between the original string and the permuted string is maximum.\n\nThe maximum Hamming Distance for a string of length \\( L \\) with all distinct characters is \\( L \\). This is because each character in the permuted string will be different from the corresponding character in the original string.\n\nTo find the number of such permutations, we need to consider the number of ways to rearrange the characters of the string. Since all characters are distinct, the number of permutations of the string is \\( L! \\) (L factorial).\n\nHowever, we need to output the result modulo \\( 1000000007 \\).\n\nHere is the Python script to solve the problem:\n\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Read input\nL = int(input())\nstring = input()\n\n# Calculate the number of permutations\nnum_permutations = math.factorial(L)\n\n# Output the result modulo 1000000007\nprint(num_permutations % 1000000007)\n```\n\nThis script reads the length of the string and the string itself. It then calculates the factorial of the length of the string, which gives the number of permutations. Finally, it prints the result modulo \\( 1000000007 \\).",
