              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Read the number of strings and the strings themselves.\n2. Generate all possible subsequences of the first string.\n3. For each subsequence, check if it is an anagram of any subsequence of the other strings.\n4. Track the longest and lexicographically smallest string that satisfies the conditions.\n5. Output the result.\n\nHere is the Python script to achieve this:\n\n```python\ndef generate_subsequences(s):\n    if len(s) == 0:\n        return ['']\n    first_char = s[0]\n    rest = s[1:]\n    without_first = generate_subsequences(rest)\n    with_first = [first_char + subseq for subseq in without_first]\n    return with_first + without_first\n\ndef is_anagram(s1, s2):\n    return sorted(s1) == sorted(s2)\n\ndef find_longest_common_subsequence(strings):\n    if not strings:\n        return ''\n    \n    # Generate all subsequences of the first string\n    subsequences = generate_subsequences(strings[0])\n    \n    # Initialize the result\n    result = ''\n    \n    for subseq in subsequences:\n        if all(is_anagram(subseq, s) for s in strings):\n            if len(subseq) > len(result) or (len(subseq) == len(result) and subseq < result):\n                result = subseq\n    \n    if result:\n        return result\n    else:\n        return 'no such string'\n\n# Read input\nN = int(input())\nstrings = [input().strip() for _ in range(N)]\n\n# Find the longest common subsequence\nresult = find_longest_common_subsequence(strings)\n\n# Output the result\nprint(result)\n```\n\nThis script defines a function `generate_subsequences` to generate all possible subsequences of a string, a function `is_anagram` to check if two strings are anagrams, and a function `find_longest_common_subsequence` to find the longest string that satisfies the conditions. Finally, it reads the input, processes it, and outputs the result.",
