              "text": "To solve this problem, we need to follow the steps described in the problem statement. Here's a step-by-step plan for the Python script:\n\n1. Read the input strings `s` and `t`.\n2. Initialize the set `A` with the string `s` and an empty set `B`.\n3. Perform the operation as described until no more operations can be performed.\n4. Count the number of operations performed.\n5. Output the maximum number of operations.\n\nLet's implement this in Python:\n\n```python\n# Read the input strings\ns = input().strip()\nt = input().strip()\n\n# Initialize the set A and B\nA = {s}\nB = set()\n\n# Function to find all subsequences of a string\ndef find_subsequences(s):\n    subsequences = {\"\"}\n    for char in s:\n        new_subsequences = set()\n        for subseq in subsequences:\n            new_subsequences.add(subseq + char)\n        subsequences.update(new_subsequences)\n    return subsequences\n\n# Function to find all subsequences of a string that match t\ndef find_matching_subsequences(s, t):\n    subsequences = find_subsequences(s)\n    matching_subsequences = {subseq for subseq in subsequences if subseq == t}\n    return matching_subsequences\n\n# Function to split the string based on the matching subsequences\ndef split_string(s, matching_subsequences):\n    splits = []\n    for subseq in matching_subsequences:\n        indices = [i for i, char in enumerate(s) if char == subseq[0]]\n        for index in indices:\n            new_splits = [s[:index]]\n            for i in range(1, len(subseq)):\n                new_splits.append(s[index + 1:index + 2])\n                index += 1\n            new_splits.append(s[index:])\n            splits.append(new_splits)\n    return splits\n\n# Perform the operation\noperations = 0\nwhile A:\n    operations += 1\n    new_A = set()\n    for u in A:\n        matching_subsequences = find_matching_subsequences(u, t)\n        if matching_subsequences:\n            splits = split_string(u, matching_subsequences)\n            for split in splits:\n                B.add(\"\".join(split))\n        else:\n            break\n    A = B\n    B = set()\n\n# Output the number of operations\nprint(operations)\n```\n\nThis script reads the input strings `s` and `t`, initializes the sets `A` and `B`, and then performs the operations as described. It uses helper functions to find all subsequences, matching subsequences, and to split the string based on the matching subsequences. The script counts the number of operations and outputs the result.",
